PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
  "round": 4,
  "consensus_status": "Split 4-2 between D (Colleagues 1, 2, 3, 5) and F (Colleagues 4, 6). Unanimous agreement that Case 2 is 'Normal development'. The debate remains solely on Case 1.",
  "key_arguments_and_evidence": {
    "pro_F_case": [
      "Colleagues 4 & 6 argue that embryogenesis is closed: ~6,000 blastoderm cells and organ systems must be built solely from maternal egg sterols before feeding on cholestanol, supposedly locking up the maternal endowment.",
      "Colleague 6 adds that ecdysteroids are catabolized and excreted at each pulse, requiring continuous dietary replenishment rather than reliance on finite maternal reserves across a 1,000-fold biomass expansion.",
      "Literal reading of the prompt: 'Adult survival is zero as well on 250mg/L cholestanol diets' is treated as an unbreakable empirical rule directly answering Question 1 as Death."
    ],
    "pro_D_rebuttals_and_evidence": [
      "Biophysical stoichiometry: An egg contains 50-100 ng sterol (mostly yolk lipid droplets). Blastoderm cellularization and embryonic organogenesis only require ~5-15 ng of structural membrane sterol. The hatched 1st instar larva still possesses >80% of maternal cholesterol, vastly exceeding the ~1-5 ng total ecdysteroid pulse requirement.",
      "Tissue remodeling/recycling: Larval tissues undergo massive histolysis during metamorphosis; membrane sterols are not irreversibly trapped.",
      "Direct grounding in foundational literature (Sang 1956; Cooke & Sang 1970; Clayton 1964; USABO source): Larvae from cholesterol-fed mothers on cholestanol alone reach adulthood normally in Gen 1. The inability of cholestanol to support life manifests in Gen 2, which then requires trace dietary cholesterol (2 mg/L) for rescue.",
      "Structural paradox of F: Question 2 explicitly specifies 'mothers that were reared on 250mg/L cholestanol'. 'Reared' strictly means raised from juvenile stages to adulthood. If Case 1 were Death, such mothers cannot exist."
    ]
  },
  "assessment": "Choice D is unequivocally supported by both experimental entomology literature and the internal textual logic of the problem. Colleagues 4 and 6 are locked into an overly literal interpretation of the preamble sentence, ignoring the experimental sequence and the meaning of 'reared'.",
  "plan_for_next_round": "Hold firm on D. Continue reinforcing the literary/experimental origin (Sang/USABO) and how the prompt's two questions mirror Gen 1 and Gen 2 of the classic sparing assay."
}

